	broken, so I needed a way to bypass that branch of MsgdirInit.
	I.e., I need to write msg files in ".", regardless of the clutter.
	The new way is a bit more flexible, and the code in main_nv.c that
	wraps around it isn't much uglier (but it's no prettier either).

Thu Oct 27 02:44:40 1994  John Salmon  (johns@mullet.anu.edu.au)

	* Make-common/Make.generic: We now allow the 'src' variable in the
	user's-makefile to contain the string PAROS, e.g., 

	  src:=foo.c bar.c PAROS-dependent.c 

	When compiled, this will turn into, e.g., :

	  src:=foo.c bar.c nx-dependent.c

	This is not activated when compiling libraries.
	  
	If the file doesn't exist you get an error.  It would be nice to
	default to "generic", but I don't see how to do that with the
	current technology.  THIS IS REALLY USEFUL.  We finally have a way
	to add PAROS specific features that aren't abstracted into the
	MPMY layer, or that violate the abstraction (e.g., enabling
	PAROS-specific counters and timers).

	* mpmy.h:  Added MPMY_Diagnostic(), which might (or might not)
	produce some useful info.  Give it a 'printf-like' arg, e.g,
	Msg_do or Shout.

	* mpmy_cm5.c:  I lied on Oct 18 when I said it was 'not too hard'.
	It's finally settling down.  The 'best' version uses a Dll of Dll's.
	The outer one lists processors that were 'jammed' the last time
	we checked.  The inner one is a list of messages that are waiting
	to go to each such processor.  A more efficient data structure is
	left to the reader...

